      "content": "```python\ndef filter_primes(nums):\n    \"\"\"Return only prime numbers from a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    def is_prime(n):\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n    return [n for n in nums if is_prime(n)]\n```\n\n**Quick comment:** This function filters a list of integers, keeping only those that are prime (numbers greater than 1 with no divisors other than 1 and themselves). It uses a helper function `is_prime` that checks divisibility up to the square root of the number for efficiency.\n\n**Example usage:**\n```python\nprint(filter_primes([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 13, 15, 17]))\n# Output: [2, 3, 5, 11, 13, 17]\n```\n\n**Time complexity:** O(n * √m), where n is the number of elements in the list and m is the maximum value in the list. This is efficient for most practical purposes. For very large lists or very large numbers, more advanced algorithms like the Sieve of Eratosthenes could be considered.\n\n**Edge cases",
